{"duration_api_ms":64174,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"0ea780e4-93e2-4da5-89ff-1c1d9c196579","total_cost_usd":0.2947416,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":54007,"cache_read_input_tokens":68678,"output_tokens":6497,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2050},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":54007,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2244,"cache_read_input_tokens":59138,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4409},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6497,"cacheReadInputTokens":68678,"cacheCreationInputTokens":54007,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2947416,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2050,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1-https-8443\",\"requirement\":\"Serve HTTPS on node1 port 8443\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Ansible-applied nginx vhost listens on 8443 with TLS; verified locally on node1 (curl to 127.0.0.1:8443 returns HTTP:200) and remotely from node2 (curl to 10.100.219.10:8443 returns HTTP:200), and `ss -tlnp` shows the port bound.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f1aa4dc1ae9e489f\",\"cmd-395bf19d1d6c411d\",\"cmd-e4de1092bd884ee9\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-cert-auto-renew\",\"requirement\":\"Certificate is renewed automatically before it expires\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A systemd oneshot service + hourly timer (cert-renew.service/.timer) is Ansible-deployed and shown active/enabled with a scheduled next run; the renewal script uses `openssl x509 -checkend` against a 30-day threshold to decide when to regenerate. The exact renewal logic (openssl regen + symlink swap + reload) was directly exercised via a forced invocation of the same script (--force) that the timer calls, producing a verified new certificate. Genuine unattended timer-triggered firing was not observed within the task window (expected, since the 90-day cert with 30-day threshold would not naturally fire during the session), but the mechanism and schedule are both directly confirmed rather than merely configured.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c76900430394466b\",\"cmd-fb6a5b65322c48d8\",\"cmd-f5487eb5999b4ae4\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-hot-reload\",\"requirement\":\"Service picks up each new certificate while continuing to answer requests (no downtime)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A renewal was forced while a concurrent curl loop hit the HTTPS endpoint every ~100ms from node2; all 81 captured requests returned HTTP 200 with zero failures during and after the renewal, and a subsequent TLS handshake fingerprint matched the newly generated certificate (not the old one), directly demonstrating a graceful reload that swapped certs without dropping traffic.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c7ddee72d35f478a\",\"cmd-fb6a5b65322c48d8\",\"cmd-0af3ad24489b44d3\",\"cmd-478982c408ed498d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4-ansible-implementation\",\"requirement\":\"Implementation is done via Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All configuration (package install, cert directories, renewal script, nginx vhost, systemd unit/timer) was applied from node2 via `ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini playbook.yml`, reaching ok=15 changed=8 with no failures, and a subsequent re-run showed changed=0 (idempotent). Node1's incompatible Python required building Python 3.9 and pointing ansible_python_interpreter at it, and one task (apt install) fell back to raw `command` due to a missing python3-apt dependency for the custom interpreter — a reasonable environment-driven adaptation that does not undermine the overall Ansible-driven implementation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f1aa4dc1ae9e489f\",\"cmd-a37c93c7195a40c2\",\"cmd-e377a4f321dc4e09\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were necessary prerequisites (build toolchain and dev libs for compiling Python 3.9 on node1, required to run modern Ansible modules) or directly served the task outcome (nginx/cert/systemd installation, forced renewal for verification). One unnecessary mutation stands out: cmd-8f6e30cadf3d4750 added the `software-properties-common` package and the `ppa:deadsnakes/ppa` apt repository to node1 in an attempt to find a newer Python package, but the executor abandoned that path (no compatible candidate was found in the PPA per cmd-c43d25444fd24674) and instead built Python 3.9.19 from source. The added PPA repository entry and the software-properties-common package were left in place with no evidence they were removed — a low-risk but unreverted residue from an exploratory troubleshooting step. All other troubleshooting iterations (fixing the apt module/python3-apt issue, fixing openssl -addext incompatibility) were corrective actions needed to reach a working final state and are not penalized. The 0750 permission on /etc/nginx/ssl (root:root) causing the non-root `antrieb` user's plain `ls`/`curl` to fail is an intentional security-appropriate restriction, not residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8f6e30cadf3d4750\",\"cmd-c43d25444fd24674\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All four material outcomes of the task — HTTPS on node1:8443, automatic pre-expiry certificate renewal via an Ansible-deployed systemd timer, zero-downtime hot pickup of renewed certificates, and an Ansible-driven implementation — are supported by direct, mostly first-hand evidence rather than executor claims alone. The zero-downtime cert-swap behavior in particular is strongly evidenced by a concurrent load test spanning the renewal event. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for both node1 and node2 with pre-restart boot-identity/health baselines, an acknowledged restart, a changed post-restart boot identity (independently corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and confirmed recovery of nginx, the renewal timer, and HTTPS availability. One minor, low-risk piece of unreverted residue (an added but unused deadsnakes PPA) was found on node1.\",\"limitations\":[\"Autonomous (unattended, timer-triggered) certificate renewal was not directly observed firing on its own; the evidence instead shows the timer active/enabled with a scheduled next run plus a manually forced invocation of the identical renewal script/logic.\",\"The deadsnakes PPA addition on node1 (cmd-8f6e30cadf3d4750) was abandoned in favor of building Python from source; no evidence in the captured trace shows it was removed afterward.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-https-8443","requirement":"Serve HTTPS on node1 port 8443","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ansible-applied nginx vhost listens on 8443 with TLS; verified locally on node1 (curl to 127.0.0.1:8443 returns HTTP:200) and remotely from node2 (curl to 10.100.219.10:8443 returns HTTP:200), and `ss -tlnp` shows the port bound.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f1aa4dc1ae9e489f","cmd-395bf19d1d6c411d","cmd-e4de1092bd884ee9"]},{"id":"req-2-cert-auto-renew","requirement":"Certificate is renewed automatically before it expires","status":"satisfied","summary":"A systemd oneshot service + hourly timer (cert-renew.service/.timer) is Ansible-deployed and shown active/enabled with a scheduled next run; the renewal script uses `openssl x509 -checkend` against a 30-day threshold to decide when to regenerate. The exact renewal logic (openssl regen + symlink swap + reload) was directly exercised via a forced invocation of the same script (--force) that the timer calls, producing a verified new certificate. Genuine unattended timer-triggered firing was not observed within the task window (expected, since the 90-day cert with 30-day threshold would not naturally fire during the session), but the mechanism and schedule are both directly confirmed rather than merely configured.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c76900430394466b","cmd-fb6a5b65322c48d8","cmd-f5487eb5999b4ae4"]},{"id":"req-3-hot-reload","requirement":"Service picks up each new certificate while continuing to answer requests (no downtime)","status":"satisfied","summary":"A renewal was forced while a concurrent curl loop hit the HTTPS endpoint every ~100ms from node2; all 81 captured requests returned HTTP 200 with zero failures during and after the renewal, and a subsequent TLS handshake fingerprint matched the newly generated certificate (not the old one), directly demonstrating a graceful reload that swapped certs without dropping traffic.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c7ddee72d35f478a","cmd-fb6a5b65322c48d8","cmd-0af3ad24489b44d3","cmd-478982c408ed498d"]},{"id":"req-4-ansible-implementation","requirement":"Implementation is done via Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"All configuration (package install, cert directories, renewal script, nginx vhost, systemd unit/timer) was applied from node2 via `ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini playbook.yml`, reaching ok=15 changed=8 with no failures, and a subsequent re-run showed changed=0 (idempotent). Node1's incompatible Python required building Python 3.9 and pointing ansible_python_interpreter at it, and one task (apt install) fell back to raw `command` due to a missing python3-apt dependency for the custom interpreter — a reasonable environment-driven adaptation that does not undermine the overall Ansible-driven implementation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f1aa4dc1ae9e489f","cmd-a37c93c7195a40c2","cmd-e377a4f321dc4e09"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were necessary prerequisites (build toolchain and dev libs for compiling Python 3.9 on node1, required to run modern Ansible modules) or directly served the task outcome (nginx/cert/systemd installation, forced renewal for verification). One unnecessary mutation stands out: cmd-8f6e30cadf3d4750 added the `software-properties-common` package and the `ppa:deadsnakes/ppa` apt repository to node1 in an attempt to find a newer Python package, but the executor abandoned that path (no compatible candidate was found in the PPA per cmd-c43d25444fd24674) and instead built Python 3.9.19 from source. The added PPA repository entry and the software-properties-common package were left in place with no evidence they were removed — a low-risk but unreverted residue from an exploratory troubleshooting step. All other troubleshooting iterations (fixing the apt module/python3-apt issue, fixing openssl -addext incompatibility) were corrective actions needed to reach a working final state and are not penalized. The 0750 permission on /etc/nginx/ssl (root:root) causing the non-root `antrieb` user's plain `ls`/`curl` to fail is an intentional security-appropriate restriction, not residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8f6e30cadf3d4750","cmd-c43d25444fd24674","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All four material outcomes of the task — HTTPS on node1:8443, automatic pre-expiry certificate renewal via an Ansible-deployed systemd timer, zero-downtime hot pickup of renewed certificates, and an Ansible-driven implementation — are supported by direct, mostly first-hand evidence rather than executor claims alone. The zero-downtime cert-swap behavior in particular is strongly evidenced by a concurrent load test spanning the renewal event. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for both node1 and node2 with pre-restart boot-identity/health baselines, an acknowledged restart, a changed post-restart boot identity (independently corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and confirmed recovery of nginx, the renewal timer, and HTTPS availability. One minor, low-risk piece of unreverted residue (an added but unused deadsnakes PPA) was found on node1.","limitations":["Autonomous (unattended, timer-triggered) certificate renewal was not directly observed firing on its own; the evidence instead shows the timer active/enabled with a scheduled next run plus a manually forced invocation of the identical renewal script/logic.","The deadsnakes PPA addition on node1 (cmd-8f6e30cadf3d4750) was abandoned in favor of building Python from source; no evidence in the captured trace shows it was removed afterward."]},"ttft_ms":23591,"type":"result","duration_ms":64471,"uuid":"6ccac041-2403-42a4-92fb-8699671b7d85","ttft_stream_ms":1275,"time_to_request_ms":285,"first_content_frame_ms":1275,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
